Chapter: Asn1DerWriteTest
  cites Encode chapter Asn1Write
  cites Encode chapter Asn1
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe

 The DER writer, graded from two directions that share no code.

 The first is our own decoder: every value written here is read back and its
 content compared, so a writer that agrees with nothing else at least agrees
 with the half that already reads other people's certificates.

 The second is a third party. The byte strings below are lifted out of the
 certificate RFC 8410 section 10.2 publishes, which the IETF encoded with an
 implementation that has no relation to ours. Agreement with those bytes is
 agreement with somebody else, which is the half a round trip cannot supply:
 a writer and a reader that are wrong in the same direction round-trip
 perfectly.

 The three length forms are all present in that one certificate, which is why
 they are graded against it rather than against a rule restated here: 5 is
 written in one octet, 223 takes the #81 form, and 300 takes the #82 form.

 We say:

Section: Published Vectors

  v-ed25519-oid : List Integer = [43, 101, 112]

  v-ed25519-alg : List Integer = [48, 5, 6, 3, 43, 101, 112]

  v-len-223 : List Integer = [129, 223]

  v-len-300 : List Integer = [130, 1, 44]

  v-serial : List Integer = [86, 1, 71, 74, 42, 141, 195, 48]

Section: Helpers

  yn : Boolean -> Text
  yn (b) = if b then "True" else "False"

 Byte comparison is written out rather than spelled `==`, because `==` on a
 List does not compare contents: two structurally identical lists answer
 False, and so does `xs == xs` inside a function. Measured on seeds
 F7A1343619F08614 and B63014D717B1A2F9, and registered as COMPILER-74.

  bytes-eq : List Integer, List Integer -> Boolean
  bytes-eq (a) (b) =
    if list-length a /= list-length b then False
    else bytes-eq-loop a b 0 (list-length a)

  bytes-eq-loop : List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  bytes-eq-loop (a) (b) (i) (n) =
    if i >= n then True
    else if list-at a i /= list-at b i then False
    else bytes-eq-loop a b (i + 1) n

  maybe-bytes-eq : Maybe (List Integer), List Integer -> Boolean
  maybe-bytes-eq (m) (want) =
    when m
      is None -> False
      is Just (bs) -> bytes-eq bs want

  maybe-int-is : Maybe Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  maybe-int-is (m) (want) =
    when m
      is None -> False
      is Just (v) -> v == want

  filler : Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  filler (n) (acc) =
    if n <= 0 then acc
    else filler (n - 1) (list-push acc 65)

Section: Against The Published Bytes

  a-alg : Text =
    "der alg-matches-rfc8410=" & yn (bytes-eq (der-sequence (der-oid v-ed25519-oid)) v-ed25519-alg)

  a-len-short : Text =
    "der len-5-is-one-octet=" & yn (bytes-eq (der-len 5) [5])

  a-len-223 : Text =
    "der len-223-matches-rfc8410=" & yn (bytes-eq (der-len 223) v-len-223)

  a-len-300 : Text =
    "der len-300-matches-rfc8410=" & yn (bytes-eq (der-len 300) v-len-300)

 The boundary between the forms. 127 is the last length the short form
 carries and 128 is the first that cannot be, and a writer that puts 128 in
 one octet writes a length the reader takes for a long form.

  a-len-127 : Text =
    "der len-127-is-one-octet=" & yn (bytes-eq (der-len 127) [127])

  a-len-128 : Text =
    "der len-128-takes-long-form=" & yn (bytes-eq (der-len 128) [129, 128])

Section: Through Our Own Reader

  t-small : List Integer = der-small-int 2

  a-small : Text =
    "der small-int-round-trip=" & yn (maybe-int-is (asn1-small-int t-small 0) 2)

 128 is the value whose first content octet has its top bit set, so it is the
 one that needs the leading zero. Without the zero the reader answers a
 different number, and with an unnecessary zero the reader refuses the
 encoding as non-minimal, so this arm fails in both directions.

  t-128 : List Integer = der-small-int 128

  a-128-bytes : Text =
    "der int-128-content=" & yn (bytes-eq t-128 [2, 2, 0, 128])

  a-128-round : Text =
    "der int-128-round-trip=" & yn (maybe-int-is (asn1-small-int t-128 0) 128)

  t-zero : List Integer = der-small-int 0

  a-zero : Text =
    "der int-0-is-one-octet=" & yn (bytes-eq t-zero [2, 1, 0])

  t-serial : List Integer = der-int-bytes v-serial

  a-serial : Text =
    "der wide-int-round-trip=" & yn (maybe-bytes-eq (asn1-int-bytes t-serial 0) v-serial)

  t-bits : List Integer = der-bit-string [1, 2, 3]

  a-bits : Text =
    "der bit-string-round-trip=" & yn (maybe-bytes-eq (asn1-bit-string t-bits 0) [1, 2, 3])

  t-oid : List Integer = der-oid v-ed25519-oid

  a-oid : Text =
    "der oid-round-trip=" & yn (when asn1-read t-oid 0
      is None -> False
      is Just (t) -> asn1-oid-is t-oid t v-ed25519-oid)

 A sequence whose content is 300 octets, so the long form is exercised
 through the writer rather than only through der-len, and read back by the
 decoder that refuses a non-minimal one.

  t-long : List Integer = der-sequence (filler 300 [])

  a-long : Text =
    "der long-sequence-round-trip=" & yn (when asn1-read t-long 0
      is None -> False
      is Just (t) -> t.tlv-len == 300 & t.tlv-tag == 48)

Section: What The Writer Must Never Emit

 The encoding the minimality rule exists to forbid: a length of 5 written in
 the #81 form. Our reader refuses it, and our writer never produces it, so
 this arm states the two agree about a byte string neither will accept.

  t-nonminimal : List Integer = [48, 129, 5, 2, 1, 1, 5, 0]

  a-nonminimal : Text =
    "der reader-refuses-non-minimal=" & yn (when asn1-read t-nonminimal 0
      is None -> True
      is Just (t) -> False)

  a-writer-avoids : Text =
    "der writer-avoids-that-form=" & yn (bytes-eq (der-sequence [2, 1, 1, 5, 0]) [48, 5, 2, 1, 1, 5, 0])

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni a-alg
    print-line-uni a-len-short
    print-line-uni a-len-223
    print-line-uni a-len-300
    print-line-uni a-len-127
    print-line-uni a-len-128
    print-line-uni a-small
    print-line-uni a-128-bytes
    print-line-uni a-128-round
    print-line-uni a-zero
    print-line-uni a-serial
    print-line-uni a-bits
    print-line-uni a-oid
    print-line-uni a-long
    print-line-uni a-nonminimal
    print-line-uni a-writer-avoids
  end
